# A Senseless World

Velan and Veni lived with their three children in grinding poverty. When Velan lost his livelihood, Veni went from house to house sweeping, cleaning, washing, and bringing home food for the children. Instead of seeing her sacrifice, Velan's distressed mind bred suspicion. Was she working only for wages, or was some shameful secret hidden there?

One day he called Veni spotless; another day he accused her of infidelity. Why had she taken so long? With whom had she smiled near the shop? Who was the man beside her? Veni trembled before questions that turned every ordinary act into guilt. She offered to stop working if he could find food for the family, but he heard even that as an insult to his manhood.

Veni consoled him: he would soon find work; when that happened she would leave all the houses and become his attendant alone. A smile touched his lips but not his heart. Suspicion returned. He followed her to each workplace, threatening imaginary men who, he claimed, looked at her wrongly. Terrified employers drove her away. Soon nobody would employ Veni, for word spread that her crazed husband might cut off someone's head.

There was no longer even half a stomachful of gruel. Velan knew inwardly that he had wronged this virtuous woman, but pride stopped his lips from asking forgiveness. Believing his presence brought her only misery, and fearing that he was losing his mind, he resolved to go far away. Others already called him mad. Then Velan disappeared.

Veni searched every street and village, asking all she met whether they had seen the father of her children, a man who would call her name. No answer came. To feed the children and continue her search, she begged for stale rice and scraps. Hunger killed without striking. She wondered where Velan had gone and whether he still lived, clutching the marriage thread at her throat as though holding it could keep her husband alive.

People turned away from her as an ill omen. Some pitied her only to pry into her story; others accused her of deceit. One woman mocked her for showing the children while asking where her husband was. Veni endured every insult for the children's sake, until dishonour and despair set her heart ablaze. She could neither protect them nor bear the degradation of begging.

In a terrible resolve, the mother who had nourished them decided not to leave them as a burden upon others. She threw the children into a pond. Two died; she took the remaining child toward another pool, intending to end that life and then her own. The hands that once guarded them from ants now extinguished their breath.

The police arrived, and a crowd gathered in the name of justice. People cried, "Can such a woman exist? Has she no heart? In this vast world was there no one to protect these children, no one to give them a handful of rice? Tear this murderess apart; throw her into a boiling cauldron!" Thus spoke those celebrated as people of feeling.

Veni answered: "Murderess, yes. I killed them; I do not deny it. You call me cruel because you do not know a mother's suffering. You say this is a very large world, and that is true. While I struggled to live, not one or two but many people told me every day, 'Why endure this nuisance? Why don't you die?' My children have now died by my hand, and I too set out to die. Only now do all of you gather and ask whether there was no place for us to live. While we are alive, you harass and threaten us and tell us to die. Once we die, you come, look, melt with pity, and ask whether there was no way to live. Until today I thought this was a wicked world. Today I know it is a senseless world."

*(Dravida Nadu, 14 January 1963)*
